# DuDuClaw OS static mounts.
#
# Root and the ESP are NOT listed here.
#
# Root comes from the kernel cmdline's root=PARTUUID=, baked into the UKI at
# build time — that is what systemd's fstab-generator turns into the sysroot
# mount in the initrd. Note this is NOT automatic: mkosi only substitutes the
# real UUID when the literal token `root=PARTUUID` appears in
# KernelCommandLine=, which it now does (mkosi.conf, H3a 2026-08-23). Before
# that the cmdline had no root= at all and root was really being chosen by
# systemd-gpt-auto-generator comparing partition labels — the wording here
# used to describe the intended design rather than the shipped one.
#
# The ESP is still auto-discovered and mounted by
# systemd-gpt-auto-generator, because its GPT partition type is the
# well-known "esp" type; setting root= disables only that generator's ROOT
# discovery, not the rest of it (its own debug output says exactly that:
# "Disabling root partition auto-detection, root= is defined", and then
# "Adding /boot: ... fstype=vfat"). An explicit fstab entry for either would
# just duplicate what already happens.
#
# It lands on **/boot, not /efi** — measured in the booted image, correcting
# what several comments in this tree used to claim. /efi exists as an empty
# directory and is NOT a mount point, which makes it a trap: a script that
# writes to /efi/... succeeds and silently puts the file on the root
# filesystem. Ask `bootctl -p` for the real path instead of hardcoding either
# name; that is also what systemd-sysupdate's PathRelativeTo=esp resolves.
#
# /data has no defined auto-mount role in the spec (Type=linux-generic,
# see mkosi.repart/30-data.conf), so it needs this explicit entry.
# Mounted by PARTLABEL — stable across A/B rebuilds since /data is never
# recreated, unlike the root partitions' PARTUUIDs which change every
# update.
#
# x-systemd.growfs re-runs the filesystem-level resize at mount time in
# case the partition itself grew (e.g. after the first-boot repart pass on
# real hardware, see mkosi.extra/etc/systemd/system/duduclaw-firstboot-repart.service)
# since a partition-table resize alone doesn't resize the ext4 filesystem
# living inside it.
PARTLABEL=duduclaw-data /data ext4 defaults,x-systemd.growfs 0 2
